From 7fc2147298a9e356fca7d9675e44b0f68d9bc3ca Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 01:21:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Node machine: C and X where they sit, at reader time, in
 the shadow

Part of #2524. After T types a full expression at reader time and takes
it (#2867), C and X run there too, on the same records, before the
unit's program exists (measure-node-machine --shadow --front-sweeps).
After the pipeline the shadow's own C and X run on the same expression,
and reader time's tape, patched with the program's symbols, must be
theirs entry for entry, with its labels, calls and temporaries, its
refusal, and the root's conversion's target and object the lowering's
pop's. A difference fails the command.

What C and X read of the program was one thing: whether an object has a
symbol, and a global's symbol. The machine holds the objects' columns
itself now (NodeMachine.object_symbols, declaration_bits, object_count),
the program's after the pipeline. At reader time an object with a static
declaration, and a string's object, has itself in its symbol's place, so
X writes the object where it writes a symbol: the entries of a global's
kinds and a direct call's row are what a splice patches. T's test of a
direct callee reads the same column.

The root's consumer comes from the front's root: no context, and a
conversion's target for an initialization alone, its object's type
without its qualifiers where the conversion writes entries.

No compile takes it. test-checker: the fixture's runs with C and X at
reader time (a const object's initializer, an empty conversion, a
static local, a global's updates, the strings, and a for statement in a
run without warnings), and the chunk-boundary text.

